WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.890
I spent years writing recommendations for government.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.480
Now I study what becomes of them.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.130
Professor Verity Marris directs the Trajectory Analytics Group.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.950
A priority starts with a good reason.

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.140
It hardens into a commitment.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:17.919
Then it keeps going,

00:00:18.019 --> 00:00:19.839
long after the reason has gone.

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:22.699
A workshop recommended a speaking token.

00:00:22.899 --> 00:00:24.809
Now twelve teams pass it round.

00:00:24.959 --> 00:00:27.729
Most of the people holding it can't say why.

00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:31.505
Detour arrows should come up when the footpath work is done.

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:34.863
Six months on, more than half are still there.

00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:37.183
Nine hundred and twenty citations,

00:00:37.333 --> 00:00:40.323
and twenty-seven outputs cited ten times or more,

00:00:40.423 --> 00:00:41.823
the second most in her school.

00:00:42.623 --> 00:00:45.177
A priority is easy to start.

00:00:45.177 --> 00:00:47.227
I want to know why it's so hard to stop.

00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:50.137
Explore her research on our website,

00:00:50.287 --> 00:00:51.647
or follow us on Bluesky.
